<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert I, Count of Flanders</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1035–<a href="/years/1093">1093</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Robert the Frisian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert the Frisian was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> from 1071 to 1093. A younger son of Baldwin V, he gained his name by marrying Gertrude of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, widow of the count of Holland, and governing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>. He seized Flanders from his nephew Arnulf III and sister-in-law Richilde at the Battle of Cassel in 1071, later made a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and was father of Robert II, a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-i-count-of-flanders.jpg" alt="Robert I, Count of Flanders" width="285" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olivier De Wree,Lambertus Vossius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_I_of_Flanders.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert was the older son of Baldwin V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and his first wife, whose name the sources do not give. His younger brother Baldwin VI succeeded their father as count in 1067, and his sister Matilda married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, duke of Normandy and later king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in 1051/2. In 1063 Robert married Gertrude of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, dowager countess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-frisia">West Frisia</a>. He gained his nickname, apparently, by acting as regent for his stepson Count Dirk V of West Frisia. Robert twice swore to renounce his claims to Flanders and received a large payment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Baldwin VI died in 1070, he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and Hainaut to his elder son Arnulf III, with Richilde, Arnulf's mother, as regent. Robert disputed the succession despite his oaths and recruited support in Maritime Flanders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>. Richilde appealed to King Philip I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, who brought an army to Flanders with the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>, Saint-Pol and Ardres. At the Battle of Cassel on 22 February 1071 Philip fled and Robert and Richilde were both captured, but Robert won. Arnulf III was among the dead. Robert claimed Flanders, and Richilde's son Baldwin became count of Hainaut. After more fighting Robert was the unchallenged ruler.</p><p>He made peace with Philip, who married his stepdaughter Bertha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-frisia">West Frisia</a>, and relations stayed friendly until Philip repudiated her in 1092. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> became a refuge for enemies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, including Edgar Ætheling in 1075. In 1080 Robert married his daughter Adela to Canute IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and in 1085 the two planned a large naval attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, which ended with Canute's assassination. Around 1077 Robert was excommunicated for failing to act against Bishop Drogo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/therouanne">Thérouanne</a>, an act probably lifted after September 1079.</p><p>In 1086 Robert made a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> with a large escort and on the way home helped the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a> against the Seljuq Turks. He died on 13 October 1093.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert I of Flanders” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Torhout, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.07,3.1&amp;year=1072" aria-label="Torhout on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Robert I, Count of Flanders"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5167 5757h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5167 5757h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.07,3.1&amp;year=1072">What was here in 1072? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
